```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10-30 rows x 13-30 cols; output same shape as input
  colors: 1 base (bg, most-common), 1 wall (2nd most-common), 3-9 marker colors; all distinct
  wall: |
    A single color partitioning the grid into 3-6 regions via axis-aligned line
    segments (horizontal/vertical). Every wall pixel has >= 1 orthogonal wall
    neighbor. Segments form T/L/cross junctions; 1-2 connected components.
    Wall pixels are unchanged in output.
  regions: |
    4-connected components of non-wall cells. All share the same base color (= bg).
    Sizes 18-350 cells. Most are rectangular; some are L/T-shaped where wall
    segments branch mid-region. Min dimension (bbox) >= 3 [observed].
  markers: |
    0-3 isolated pixels per region, each a distinct non-base, non-wall color.
    Placed at consecutive Chebyshev distances from region boundary: typically
    d=0,1,2 (on a diagonal from a region corner). Each distance has exactly one
    marker. All marker colors within a region are distinct.
  palette_rule: |
    Sort markers by Chebyshev distance. If min distance > 0, prepend base color.
    Palette = [base if needed] + [marker colors in distance order].
    Regions with 0 markers get palette = [base] (no change).
  output_rule: |
    For each region cell, paint palette[chebyshev_dist_from_boundary % len(palette)].
    Produces concentric rectangular frames alternating palette colors inward.

generation_strategy: |
  1. Pick grid size (H x W), bg color, wall color, and a pool of marker colors.
  2. Design wall: place 1-3 axis-aligned line segments (full rows/cols or partial)
     creating 3-6 connected non-wall regions. Ensure each region has min dim >= 3.
  3. For each region, choose 0-3 marker colors (distinct, not bg/wall). Place the
     first marker at a corner of the region (Chebyshev dist 0), subsequent markers
     one diagonal step inward (dist 1, 2, ...). Ensure max marker dist < region's
     max Chebyshev depth. Optionally omit dist-0 marker so base leads the palette.
  4. For regions with 0 markers, output = all base (no visible change).
  5. Validate: run solver (transform) on input and confirm output matches expected
     concentric-frame pattern.
```
